{%- comment -%}
  PG R36S Mobile: one swipeable gallery, the orb page's running order, and an
  ADD TO CART inside every offer tile.

  Registered in templates/product.pg-landing.json only (the R36S). Every visual
  rule is ≤900px — desktop keeps the gallery and the column it has today.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------- THE GALLERY

  pg-landing renders three separate things: .pgx-hero (the first image),
  .pgx-vid (the clip from the section's "Gallery video URL" setting) and
  .pgx-thumbwrap (every other image). On a phone that reads as one big photo,
  then a video, then a wall of pictures.

  On phones they are now ONE carousel, like the orb: the strip is the gallery,
  one item fills the view, and a "‹ SWIPE · n/N ›" pill on the photo says how
  far along you are. Nothing is dropped — the hero photo is copied in as the
  first slide, the video is MOVED in as the second, and the rest follow in
  their product order.

  Copied, not moved, for the hero: pg-landing's own thumbnail clicks write to
  #pgx-hero-img, so moving that node into the strip would give the shopper a
  slide whose picture changes under them. A copy takes the hero's src once, at
  build time, and then stands still. The hero container is hidden only once the
  copy is in place (the html.pg-r36-gal class), so a failure leaves the page
  looking exactly as it does today rather than with no photo at all.

  The video IS moved, since a second <video> would download the clip twice, and
  it leaves a placeholder behind so rotating past 900px puts it back where
  pg-landing had it. The desktop DOM is restored on that same pass.

  The pill is pg-gallery-tweaks' markup and styling restated: that file is
  registered on the orb template only, so the class names cannot collide here,
  and repeating them is what makes the two pages look identical. The counter
  repaints on every animation frame while the strip scrolls, not on a debounce,
  so it counts up under the finger instead of jumping when the strip settles.

  ------------------------------------------------------------ THE BUY COLUMN

  The orb's phone layout comes from pg-tiktok-pdp, which turns .pgx-buy into a
  flex column and gives each child an explicit order. The R36S had pg-landing's
  plain DOM order — the review carousel directly under the stars, above the
  bundle. The same order values are restated so both pages read:

      title | stars | BUNDLE & SAVE | tiles | add to cart | sellout risk
      | review carousel | info row | payment | guarantee | accordions

  Order, not DOM moves: pg-mobile's quoteUp() re-seats the review card after the
  rating every 1.5s and would fight a relocation. pg-sellout-last (also on this
  template) is what puts the sellout card under the last tile; this file only
  gives its wrapper the seat.

  --------------------------------------------------- AN ADD TO CART PER TILE

  Like the orb, where pg-tiktok-pdp puts one in every ladder tile: visible only
  in the selected tile, with the big bottom button hidden on phones so exactly
  one button is on screen and it belongs to the offer being looked at.

  It must cover BOTH of this page's tiles, which come from different places and
  share only .pgx-tile: the Single Item tile is pg-landing's own, and the Duo
  Pack is built by pgDuo (pg-theme-css) as `.pgx-tile.pgx-duo`. An earlier pass
  keyed on pg-landing's [data-pgx-tile] attribute and so skipped the Duo Pack
  entirely — the selector here is every .pgx-tile in the buy column.

  A click clicks #pgx-atc. For the SINGLE tile each owner takes it from there as
  before. For the DUO PACK this file now owns the click outright -- see the note
  on THE DUO PACK ADDS EXACTLY TWO further down. The button is a SPAN with
  role=button (the base theme paints real buttons with a dark ::before overlay)
  and deliberately does NOT carry .pgx-atc, or the handlers would fire from the
  in-tile button itself and double-post. It only appears in the selected tile,
  so by the time it can be pressed the tile it belongs to is already the one
  that will be sold.
{%- endcomment -%}
<style>
  /* off at every width except phones, where the rules below turn them on */
  #pgx .pg-gt-swipe{display:none}
  #pgx .pgx-tile .pg-r36-atc{display:none}

  @media (max-width:900px){
    /* ---- one carousel: hero copy, then the clip, then every other photo ---- */
    html.pg-r36-gal #pgx .pgx-hero{display:none !important}
    #pgx .pgx-thumbwrap{display:block !important;position:relative !important;margin-top:0 !important}
    #pgx .pgx-thumbwrap .pgx-thumbs{
      display:flex !important;flex-wrap:nowrap !important;
      overflow-x:auto !important;scroll-behavior:smooth;
      scroll-snap-type:x mandatory;
      gap:10px !important;margin-top:0 !important;
      scrollbar-width:none;-ms-overflow-style:none;-webkit-overflow-scrolling:touch;
    }
    #pgx .pgx-thumbwrap .pgx-thumbs::-webkit-scrollbar{display:none}
    #pgx .pgx-thumbwrap .pgx-thumbs > *{flex:0 0 100% !important;scroll-snap-align:center}
    #pgx .pgx-thumbwrap .pgx-thumbs button{padding:0;border:0;background:#0D0714;border-radius:10px;overflow:hidden;cursor:pointer}
    #pgx .pgx-thumbwrap .pgx-thumbs button img{aspect-ratio:1/1 !important;object-fit:cover !important;width:100% !important;height:auto !important}
    #pgx .pgx-thumbwrap .pgx-thumbs .pgx-vid{margin:0 !important;border-radius:10px;overflow:hidden;background:#0d0d0d}
    #pgx .pgx-thumbwrap .pgx-thumbs .pgx-vid video{
      width:100% !important;display:block;
      aspect-ratio:1/1 !important;object-fit:cover !important;
      height:auto !important;max-height:none !important;
    }
    /* phones swipe; the arrows sat on top of the picture */
    #pgx .pgx-tharrow{display:none !important}

    /* ---- the swipe pill, as on the orb ---- */
    #pgx .pgx-thumbwrap .pg-gt-swipe{
      display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;gap:10px;
      position:absolute;bottom:10px;top:auto;left:50%;transform:translateX(-50%);
      z-index:4;margin:0;padding:11px 20px;
      width:max-content;max-width:100%;
      border-radius:999px;
      background:rgba(11,7,16,.82);
      border:1px solid rgba(190,140,235,.55);
      box-shadow:0 6px 18px -6px rgba(0,0,0,.8);
      color:#EEE1FB;
      font-family:'Poppins','Segoe UI',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;
      font-size:15px;font-weight:800;
      letter-spacing:.10em;text-transform:uppercase;
      white-space:nowrap;pointer-events:none;
    }
    #pgx .pg-gt-swipe span{display:inline-block;font-size:23px;line-height:.7;color:#F6ECFF;animation:pgGtNudge 1.5s ease-in-out infinite}
    #pgx .pg-gt-swipe .pg-gt-l{animation-direction:reverse}
    /* fixed width, so the centred pill does not shimmy as the digits change */
    #pgx .pg-gt-swipe b.pg-gt-n{
      display:inline-block;min-width:4.8ch;text-align:center;
      font-weight:800;color:#FFFFFF;letter-spacing:.04em;
      font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;
    }

    /* ---- the orb page's running order ---- */
    #pgx .pgx-buy{display:flex;flex-direction:column}
    #pgx .pgx-buy > *{order:7}
    #pgx .pgx-buy > .pgx-title{order:1}
    #pgx .pgx-buy > .pgx-rating{order:2}
    #pgx .pgx-buy > .pgx-bs{order:3}
    #pgx .pgx-buy > .pgx-divider{order:3}
    #pgx .pgx-buy > .pgx-tile{order:4}
    #pgx .pgx-buy > .pgx-deal-note{order:5}
    #pgx .pgx-buy > .pgx-atc{order:5}
    #pgx .pgx-buy > .pgx-err{order:6}
    #pgx .pgx-buy > .pgx-quote{order:8;margin-top:16px}
    #pgx .pgx-buy > .pgx-sellout{order:9}
    #pgx .pgx-buy > .pgx-info{order:10}
    #pgx .pgx-buy > .pg-payrow{order:11}
    #pgx .pgx-buy > .pgx-pay{order:11}
    #pgx .pgx-buy > .pgx-guar{order:12}
    #pgx .pgx-buy > .pgx-acc{order:13}

    /* ---- an ADD TO CART inside the selected tile ---- */
    #pgx .pgx-tile.pgx-sel .pg-r36-atc{
      display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;gap:8px;
      margin-top:12px;padding:15px 12px;
      border-radius:10px;
      /* the same moving purple as every other button on the site (pgShiftT
         is declared in pg-theme-css), not a static fill. 8s, not 5s, since
         2026-09-11: the shop owner found the wave "too dynamic", so every
         ADD TO CART button on the site now runs the same slower cycle (see
         paint() in pg-theme-css). The orb page's copy of this button, in
         pg-orb-atc, is kept in step by hand. */
      background-image:linear-gradient(120deg,#9B4BC4 0%,#7A2FA2 30%,#4A1C66 50%,#7A2FA2 70%,#9B4BC4 100%);
      background-color:#7A2FA2;
      background-size:240% 240%;
      animation:pgShiftT 8s ease-in-out infinite;
      color:#FFFFFF;
      font-family:'Poppins','Segoe UI',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;
      font-size:15.5px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;
      text-align:center;cursor:pointer;
      box-shadow:0 14px 30px -12px rgba(139,68,190,.75);
      -webkit-user-select:none;user-select:none;
      transition:filter .15s ease,opacity .15s ease;
    }
    #pgx .pgx-tile.pgx-sel .pg-r36-atc:hover{filter:brightness(1.1)}
    #pgx .pgx-tile.pgx-sel .pg-r36-atc.busy{opacity:.6;cursor:default}
    #pgx .pgx-tile.pgx-sel .pg-r36-atc:after{content:"\2192";font-size:17px;line-height:1}
    /* the bottom button duplicates the in-tile one on phones. Browsers without
       :has() simply keep both, which is harmless. */
    #pgx .pgx-buy:has(.pgx-tile .pg-r36-atc) > .pgx-atc{display:none !important}
  }

  @keyframes pgGtNudge{
    0%,100%{transform:translateX(0);opacity:.55}
    50%    {transform:translateX(4px);opacity:1}
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){
    #pgx .pg-gt-swipe span{animation:none}
  }
</style>
<script>
(function(){
  if (window.pgR36Mobile) return; window.pgR36Mobile = 1;

  /* Claimed before anything else runs: pg-theme-css reads this to stand its own
     Duo Pack handler and its add-on ride-along down, so the tile has exactly
     one writer even on a browser where stopImmediatePropagation does not save
     us. See THE DUO PACK ADDS EXACTLY TWO below. */
  window.pgDuoOwner = 1;

  function mobile(){ return !window.matchMedia || window.matchMedia('(max-width: 900px)').matches; }

  /* ---------------- the carousel ---------------- */

  var HEROSRC = '';   /* read once: thumbnail clicks rewrite #pgx-hero-img */

  function gallery(){
    var wrap = document.querySelector('#pgx .pgx-thumbwrap');
    if (!wrap) return;
    var strip = wrap.querySelector('.pgx-thumbs');
    if (!strip) return;

    if (mobile()){
      var hero = document.getElementById('pgx-hero-img');
      if (hero && !HEROSRC) HEROSRC = hero.getAttribute('src') || '';
      if (HEROSRC && !strip.querySelector('.pg-r36-slide')){
        var b = document.createElement('button');
        b.type = 'button';
        b.className = 'pg-r36-slide';
        var im = document.createElement('img');
        im.src = HEROSRC;
        im.alt = (hero && hero.getAttribute('alt')) || '';
        b.appendChild(im);
        strip.insertBefore(b, strip.firstChild);
      }
      /* the clip becomes the second slide, leaving a marker in its old seat */
      var vid = document.querySelector('#pgx .pgx-vid');
      if (vid && vid.parentNode !== strip){
        if (!vid.pgR36Ph){
          var ph = document.createElement('i');
          ph.className = 'pg-r36-ph';
          ph.hidden = true;
          vid.parentNode.insertBefore(ph, vid);
          vid.pgR36Ph = ph;
        }
        var lead = strip.querySelector('.pg-r36-slide');
        strip.insertBefore(vid, lead ? lead.nextSibling : strip.firstChild);
      }
      if (strip.querySelector('.pg-r36-slide')) document.documentElement.classList.add('pg-r36-gal');
      pill(wrap, strip);
    } else {
      /* rotated back to desktop: put pg-landing's gallery back as it was */
      var v2 = strip.querySelector('.pgx-vid');
      if (v2 && v2.pgR36Ph && v2.pgR36Ph.parentNode){
        v2.pgR36Ph.parentNode.insertBefore(v2, v2.pgR36Ph);
        v2.pgR36Ph.parentNode.removeChild(v2.pgR36Ph);
        v2.pgR36Ph = null;
      }
      var s2 = strip.querySelector('.pg-r36-slide');
      if (s2 && s2.parentNode) s2.parentNode.removeChild(s2);
      document.documentElement.classList.remove('pg-r36-gal');
    }
  }

  function pill(wrap, strip){
    var el = wrap.querySelector('.pg-gt-swipe');
    if (!el){
      if (strip.children.length < 2) return;      /* nothing to swipe to */
      el = document.createElement('div');
      el.className = 'pg-gt-swipe';
      el.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
      el.innerHTML = '<span class="pg-gt-l">&lsaquo;</span> Swipe &middot; <b class="pg-gt-n"></b> <span>&rsaquo;</span>';
      wrap.appendChild(el);
    }
    paint();
  }

  /* which slide is in view. Synchronous and allocation-light: safe to call once
     per animation frame while the strip is being swiped. */
  function paint(){
    var wrap = document.querySelector('#pgx .pgx-thumbwrap');
    if (!wrap) return;
    var strip = wrap.querySelector('.pgx-thumbs');
    var cnt = wrap.querySelector('.pg-gt-swipe .pg-gt-n');
    if (!strip || !cnt) return;
    var kids = strip.children;
    if (!kids.length) return;
    var mid = strip.scrollLeft + strip.clientWidth / 2;
    var best = 0, bestD = Infinity;
    for (var i = 0; i < kids.length; i++){
      var k = kids[i];
      var d = Math.abs((k.offsetLeft + k.offsetWidth / 2) - mid);
      if (d < bestD){ bestD = d; best = i; }
    }
    var pos = (best + 1) + '/' + kids.length;
    if (cnt.textContent !== pos) cnt.textContent = pos;
  }

  /* ---------------- an ADD TO CART in every tile ---------------- */

  function buttons(){
    /* every tile in the buy column: pg-landing's Single Item AND pgDuo's
       Duo Pack, which share only .pgx-tile */
    document.querySelectorAll('#pgx .pgx-buy > .pgx-tile').forEach(function(tile){
      if (tile.querySelector('.pg-r36-atc')) return;
      var b = document.createElement('span');
      b.className = 'pg-r36-atc';
      b.setAttribute('role', 'button');
      b.setAttribute('tabindex', '0');
      b.textContent = 'Add to cart';
      b.addEventListener('click', function(){
        if (b.classList.contains('busy')) return;
        var atc = document.getElementById('pgx-atc');
        if (!atc) return;
        b.classList.add('busy');
        b.textContent = 'Adding…';
        /* whichever tile is selected owns the click: the Duo Pack is taken by
           the window-capture owner below, pg-landing's own handler posts the
           single variant otherwise */
        atc.click();
        setTimeout(function(){ b.classList.remove('busy'); b.textContent = 'Add to cart'; }, 2600);
      });
      b.addEventListener('keydown', function(e){
        if (e.key === 'Enter' || e.key === ' '){ e.preventDefault(); b.click(); }
      });
      tile.appendChild(b);
    });
  }

  function pass(){ gallery(); buttons(); }

  /* ================= THE DUO PACK ADDS EXACTLY TWO =================
     Reported: adding the Duo Pack puts THREE in the cart and the drawer starts
     glitching. Measured in Chromium against the real handler shapes, not guessed.

     pgDuo posts the two consoles and then calls `e.stopPropagation()`. That is
     the bug. stopPropagation stops the event travelling to the NEXT node -- it
     does nothing about other listeners already bound to the SAME node, and
     pg-theme-css binds three separate `.pgx-atc` click handlers on `document`
     in the capture phase. Reproduced:

         click -> pgDuo (+2 consoles) , rider (+N cases)     <- both ran

     The second is the add-on rider at pg-theme-css:1303, which posts the spare
     case with `quantity: unitsOf(sel)` 900ms later on its own timer. So one tap
     becomes two independent /cart/add.js calls plus pg-giftguard's free case --
     three writes to the cart in under a second, each triggering its own drawer
     refresh. That is both the extra item and the glitching: the drawer is being
     rebuilt from three different answers while the shopper watches.

     (pg-landing's handler is bound on #pgx-atc itself and IS correctly stopped,
     confirmed in the same test -- so the console count comes from pgDuo alone.)

     THE FIX IS ONE OWNER FOR THAT ONE CLICK. This listener is on WINDOW in the
     capture phase. Window is the first node in the propagation path, so it runs
     before every document-level listener no matter who registered first --
     pg-case-add already relies on exactly this and says so. When the Duo tile is
     the selected one it takes the click outright with stopImmediatePropagation,
     posts ONE request, and neither pgDuo nor the rider ever sees it.

     Scoped as narrowly as it can be: the console page, and only while
     .pgx-duo.pgx-sel exists. Every other tile and every other page keeps the
     behaviour it has today -- verified: with the duo NOT selected the two theme
     handlers still fire exactly as before.

     The spare case is still honoured if its tickbox is ticked, in the same
     request, so ticking it cannot race the console add either. */
  var SPARE_CASE = 49640879063268;   /* r36s-protective-case-free-gift / "Spare case" */
  var duoBusy = false;

  function duoTile(){
    if (!/\/products\/handheld-game-console/.test(location.pathname)) return null;
    /* either pack tile: the trio is built by the same section as the duo and
       posts through this one handler, so a third console can never come from
       a second writer */
    return document.querySelector('#pgx .pgx-tile.pgx-duo.pgx-sel, #pgx .pgx-tile.pgx-trio.pgx-sel');
  }

  window.addEventListener('click', function(e){
    if (!e.target || !e.target.closest) return;
    if (!e.target.closest('.pgx-atc')) return;
    var duo = duoTile();
    if (!duo) return;                       /* not the Duo Pack: leave the theme alone */

    e.preventDefault();
    e.stopPropagation();
    if (e.stopImmediatePropagation) e.stopImmediatePropagation();
    if (duoBusy) return;                    /* a second tap cannot double-add */
    duoBusy = true;

    /* one picker per console in the tile: two on the duo, three on the trio */
    var sels = duo.querySelectorAll('select');
    var ids = Array.prototype.map.call(sels, function(s){ return parseInt(s.value, 10) || 0; });
    if (ids.length < 2 || ids.some(function(id){ return !id; })){ duoBusy = false; return; }

    /* ONE DUO PACK LINE, NOT TWO CONSOLE LINES.

       This used to post the two console variants and let Shopify's
       "R36S - 2nd Console 25% Off" BXGY find the discount. That engine always
       discounts the LOWEST-priced eligible line and splits the pack across
       lines of its own choosing, which is where the whole family of bugs came
       from: three consoles in the cart, the tile disagreeing with the till,
       the 25% landing on a console the shopper did not pick, and a cent
       wandering between lines whenever the line count changed.

       The pack is its own product now, one variant per configuration, priced
       at exactly the figure the tile prints. Nothing is computed at checkout,
       so the cart charges what the page promised and adding two consoles
       separately earns no bundle price -- which is the intended behaviour. */
    /* ONE PACK VARIANT PER CONFIGURATION, KEYED ON THE SORTED SIZES.
       Sorting is what makes 64+128 and 128+64 the same key, so a mixed duo
       always resolves to the surviving 64GB+128GB variant and never to the
       retired 128GB+64GB duplicate (49831405846756: priced $2.50 apart from
       its twin until 2026-09-10, now equal, kept only so old links resolve).
       Prices here are the 25 / 32 / 38 ladder set that day. */
    var PACKS = {
      '64|64':        49831383531748,   /* Duo  $135.98 */
      '64|128':       49831383564516,   /* Duo  $148.98 */
      '128|128':      49831383597284,   /* Duo  $161.98 */
      '64|64|64':     49835591270628,   /* Trio $185.97 */
      '64|64|128':    49835591565540,   /* Trio $197.97 */
      '64|128|128':   49835591598308,   /* Trio $209.97 */
      '128|128|128':  49835591631076    /* Trio $221.97 */
    };
    var SIZE_OF = {};
    SIZE_OF[49622431858916] = '64';
    SIZE_OF[49622431826148] = '128';

    var sizes = ids.map(function(id){ return SIZE_OF[id] || ''; });
    if (sizes.some(function(z){ return !z; })){ duoBusy = false; return; }
    sizes.sort(function(a, b){ return a - b; });
    var packId = PACKS[sizes.join('|')];
    if (!packId){ duoBusy = false; return; }   /* unknown configuration: add nothing */

    var items = [{id: packId, quantity: 1}];

    /* and the spare case only if the shopper actually ticked it -- same request,
       so it cannot race the console add the way the rider's 900ms timer did */
    /* ONE spare, not two: the pack already includes a free case, so the second
       console is the only one uncovered. Matches the row's own price now. */
    /* one free case per order, so spares = consoles minus one: 1 on the duo, 2 on the trio */
    if (duo.querySelector('.pg-tadd.on, .pg-uacc.on')) items.push({id: SPARE_CASE, quantity: Math.max(1, ids.length - 1)});

    /* AND THE COUNT IS MADE TRUE AFTERWARDS.
       Owning the click stops every writer this theme has, and the two handlers
       that could add a third console -- pg-landing's single and pgDuo's own duo
       -- are now stood down by name rather than by event ordering. That should
       be the whole fix. It is not trusted to be: three-in-cart was reported
       from a device whose extra write nothing in these files explains, and a
       shopper being charged for a console they did not ask for is not a bug to
       leave riding on an argument about propagation.

       So the promise is enforced: the count the cart held BEFORE this tap, plus
       two. The cart is then re-read on a short schedule that outlasts every
       late writer in the theme (pg-theme-css's 900ms add-on rider,
       pg-giftguard's beat, a drawer refresh), and any surplus console is taken
       back off.

       THE BASELINE IS READ BEFORE THE CLICK, NOT DURING IT. A /cart.js issued
       alongside the add is not a baseline: a stray write from this same tap can
       reach the server first and be counted, which would raise the target to
       three and license the very thing being prevented. lastN below is polled
       on its own beat and is therefore always a strictly-earlier reading. If it
       has not been read yet, nothing is enforced -- an unenforced add is far
       better than one trimmed against a number that might be wrong.

       Deliberately meek: it only ever REMOVES, only ever touches the console
       line, never takes off more than the two units this tap added, never
       leaves fewer than the pack's two, and stops as soon as the count agrees.
       When the total is already right -- every case that could be measured --
       it issues no write. */
    /* NOTHING TO TRIM ANY MORE. The enforcement above was written when the
       Duo was two console lines; the pack is one line of its own product now,
       so the only time trimConsoles() could ever write was on a stale
       baseline, and then it removed a console the shopper had put there
       themselves. Left in place but disarmed (2026-09-11). */
    var target = null;

    fetch('/cart/add.js', {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: {'Content-Type': 'application/json'},
      credentials: 'same-origin',
      body: JSON.stringify({items: items})
    }).then(function(r){
      duoBusy = false;
      if (!r.ok) return;
      if (window.pgOpenCart) window.pgOpenCart();
      /* three checks, not five: each is a read on an endpoint five other
         sections are already polling, and the last one is well past every
         late writer in the theme */
      [1200, 2500, 4000].forEach(function(t){
        setTimeout(function(){ if (target !== null) trimConsoles(target); }, t);
      });
    }).catch(function(){ duoBusy = false; });
  }, true);

  function consolesIn(cart){
    var n = 0;
    (cart.items || []).forEach(function(it){
      if (it.handle === 'handheld-game-console') n += it.quantity;
    });
    return n;
  }

  /* The console count as it stood BEFORE the current tap.

     ON A LONG BEAT, AND NOT WHILE THE DRAWER IS OPEN. /cart.js is shared with
     pg-drawer, pg-cart-total, pg-cart-variants, pg-chips and pg-giftguard, and
     Shopify throttles it under their combined load -- pg-chips documents
     responses coming back unparseable, and a stale read there is what let
     pg-giftguard add duplicate cases. A baseline for a button the shopper has
     not pressed yet does not need to be a second old, so this asks every 5s,
     and not at all while the cart drawer is open (the tile cannot be tapped
     from behind it). Closing the drawer refreshes it immediately, so an edit
     made in there is picked up before the next tap rather than 5s later. */
  var lastN = null;
  function watch(){
    if (!/\/products\/handheld-game-console/.test(location.pathname)) return;
    if (duoBusy) return;                    /* mid-add: not a baseline any more */
    if (document.hidden) return;
    var cart = document.getElementById('cart');
    if (cart && /(^|\s)toggle(\s|$)/.test(cart.className)) return;   /* drawer open */
    fetch('/cart.js', {headers: {'X-Requested-With': 'XMLHttpRequest'}})
      .then(function(r){ return r.json(); })
      .then(function(c){ if (!duoBusy) lastN = consolesIn(c); })
      .catch(function(){});
  }

  /* the drawer closing is the one moment the baseline is most likely stale */
  function watchDrawer(){
    var cart = document.getElementById('cart');
    if (!cart || !window.MutationObserver) return;
    var was = /(^|\s)toggle(\s|$)/.test(cart.className);
    new MutationObserver(function(){
      var now = /(^|\s)toggle(\s|$)/.test(cart.className);
      if (was && !now) watch();
      was = now;
    }).observe(cart, {attributes: true, attributeFilter: ['class']});
  }

  /* Take the surplus off the last console line. Never adds, never touches
     another product, never removes more than the two units this tap added, and
     returns without a write whenever the count already agrees -- the normal
     case, so the repeated checks cost one GET each and nothing more. */
  var trimBusy = false;
  function trimConsoles(target){
    if (trimBusy) return;
    trimBusy = true;
    fetch('/cart.js', {headers: {'X-Requested-With': 'XMLHttpRequest'}})
      .then(function(r){ return r.json(); })
      .then(function(c){
        var lines = (c.items || []).filter(function(it){ return it.handle === 'handheld-game-console'; });
        var total = 0;
        lines.forEach(function(it){ total += it.quantity; });
        if (total <= target || !lines.length){ trimBusy = false; return; }
        /* capped at the two units this tap is answerable for, so a stale
           baseline can never eat a console the shopper put there themselves */
        var surplus = Math.min(total - target, 2);
        var last = lines[lines.length - 1];
        var q = Math.max(0, last.quantity - surplus);
        return fetch('/cart/change.js', {
          method: 'POST',
          headers: {'Content-Type': 'application/json'},
          credentials: 'same-origin',
          body: JSON.stringify({id: last.key, quantity: q})
        }).then(function(){
          trimBusy = false;
          if (window.pgDrawerRefresh){ try { return window.pgDrawerRefresh(); } catch (e){} }
          if (window.pgOpenCart) window.pgOpenCart();
        });
      })
      .catch(function(){ trimBusy = false; });
  }

  function boot(){
    pass();
    /* the strip's own scrolling: `scroll` does not bubble but it does capture,
       and listening on the document survives the gallery being rebuilt */
    var raf = 0;
    document.addEventListener('scroll', function(e){
      var t = e.target;
      if (!t || !t.classList || !t.classList.contains('pgx-thumbs')) return;
      if (!raf) raf = requestAnimationFrame(function(){ raf = 0; paint(); });
    }, {capture: true, passive: true});

    var root = document.getElementById('pgx');
    if (root && window.MutationObserver){
      var q = false;
      /* pgDuo rebuilds its tile on an interval, and the gallery can be
         re-rendered under us; every pass is idempotent */
      new MutationObserver(function(){
        if (q) return;
        q = true;
        setTimeout(function(){ q = false; pass(); }, 80);
      }).observe(root, {childList: true, subtree: true});
    }
    window.addEventListener('resize', function(){ pass(); paint(); }, {passive: true});
    watch();
    watchDrawer();
    setInterval(watch, 5000);
    setInterval(pass, 900);
    [300, 900, 2000, 4000].forEach(function(t){ setTimeout(function(){ pass(); paint(); }, t); });
  }
  if (document.readyState === 'loading') document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', boot); else boot();
})();
</script>
